test(10-keeler-top): split the stream at its last newline, not by hand

The mutation gate found three timeouts and no survivors, all on one
line: `consumed += newline + 1`, the index advance in the line-splitting
loop. Every arithmetic mutation of it — `*=`, `+ newline - 1`,
`+ newline * 1` — leaves the index where it was or sends it backwards,
so the loop never ends. A test suite cannot report that as a failure;
it can only hang, and a gate that reads a hang has learned nothing about
whether the tests would catch the bug.

So the arithmetic goes. `rposition` finds the last newline, everything
up to and including it is `split` into whole lines, and the tail stays
in the buffer for the next poll. Same answer, no index to slip, and the
three timeouts become no mutants at all.

The fix is the shape the rules ask for — a stronger test would not have
helped here, because the tests were already strong enough to catch a
wrong answer and the mutant did not produce one.
